All tileset versions do that, including the one that ships
with 3.4.3.  (There are a couple of exceptions.  The 4
types of gray stone, for example, all appear identical.)

> I have not found it possible using the original text interface

The ; command can give you almost all of the same
information. It's more tedious to use if you actually
need to check *every* object, but experienced players
usually only have to check a few things here and there.
Most of the time we can predict what ; would tell us --
well, except for the identity of random weapons laying
around on the ground, but we usually don't *need* to
know all of that, because it's junk.  It doesn't generally
matter if the orc dropped a scimitar or a stack of orcish
daggers, for example, if you are already wielding a
long sword.

Besides that, even tilesets only tell you the one item
on top, so if you are doing an exhaustive search or
want to be sure you're not missing goodies (potions,
for example, in a death drop pile), you have to step
on the tile anyway.

> - Vulture is using special patched version of
>  nethack that gives unfair information.
>
> What is your opinion on this subject?

Unless it gives the player information that the official
tileset version does not, I think the relevance of the
special patch can be written off here.

I do think tilesets are easier for a beginner to play with.
I learned using the Qt version myself.  It makes it easier,
when you don't yet have a full inventory of all the game's
monsters and objects in your head, to notice when you see
something you haven't seen before, to learn to recognize
common dangers, and to begin the process of _building_ in
your head a library of the games various monsters and
objects.  I would recommend that new players use a tileset
for at least a week, maybe longer, before attempting to
play with the tty interface (unless you are specifically
looking for a hardcore experience).

Of course you will eventually want to switch to the tty
interface.  Among other reasons, this means you can play
on a public server and take advantage of the automatic
ttyrec feature, and the recordings when you watch them
will look just like the game you're used to playing.
Re-watching your games is a very useful learning tool.
It would of course be *possible*, modern disk sizes being
what they are, to use full-bore desktop recording software
(such as is used to create tutorials for how to use various
software) to manually record all your own play.  But that
would simultaneously be tedious and also not provide all
the benefits of a public server, such as the ability to
ask other people on RGRN to have a look at one of your
ttyrecs and make suggestions about what you did wrong,
which can be rather instructive.


